attlefield", "No public general-election polling; prediction markets price a Republican win near 83 percent"], "rationale": "The district's eight-point Republican lean, Knott's incumbency, and a Likely-to-Solid R consensus from ten raters outweigh Barringer's strong fundraising and national Democratic targeting, while markets price Knott at roughly an 83 percent win probability.", "watch": "A deepening national environment against Republicans, sustained outside spending for Barringer, or slipping Trump approval in North Carolina's red districts could compress the margin into tossup territory.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Brad Knott (R) is running."]}, "overview": "North Carolina's 13th District leans Republican by about eight points, and first-term Representative Brad Knott returns as the incumbent after clearing his primary. Ten expert ratings split between Likely R and Solid or Safe R, with none placing the seat beyond Likely R, and prediction markets price Knott's chances near 83 percent. No general-election polling is available.\n\nDemocrat Paul Barringer has run a well-funded challenge, raising just over one million dollars, and national Democrats have added the district to their target list. Coverage of softening Trump approval in some of North Carolina's reddest seats gives Democrats a potential opening if the midterm environment deepens. Still, the partisan lean, Knott's incumbency and legislative record, and the absence of any polling showing a genuine contest point to a Republican hold, with Barringer needing a strong national wave to close an eight-point gap.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0835, "margin": -8.64}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0743, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.1064, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.1381, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0942, "margin": -8.